@[system]

※※ Clocks: what time it is by each the system keeps, and how finely each tells it.
※
※ Imported by `std` and handed on, so a program knows it as `std.time`.  The two
※ functions are POSIX's `clock_gettime` and `clock_getres`, answering the time
※ or the system's error number, and `Timespec` is C's `struct timespec`:
※ seconds, and nanoseconds within the second.  A program reads the clocks
※ through the functions the kernel maps into every process, the vDSO, which
※ answer without entering the kernel: where they are is found once, through
※ the auxiliary vector, by the initializers of `constinit` variables, which run
※ before the program does and leave them read-only -- in a program that reads
※ a clock, and in no other.  Where there is no vDSO -- a shared object, which
※ never sees the vector -- the kernel is asked.

※※ A time, as C's `struct timespec` holds one.
※※
※※ \details `tv_sec` is whole seconds and `tv_nsec` the nanoseconds past them,
※※ from nought to 999999999.  Which seconds they are counted from is the
※※ clock's: the epoch of 1970 for `Clock.realtime`, an unspecified moment for
※※ the others.
@[export, abi]
type Timespec = tv_sec : i64 ; tv_nsec : i64

※※ The clocks Linux keeps, with the numbers its kernel gives them, which are the
※※ same on every architecture.
※※
※※ \details `realtime` is the time of day, which may be set and may jump;
※※ `monotonic` never goes back and does not count a suspended system;
※※ `boottime` is `monotonic` that does; `monotonic_raw` is `monotonic` without
※※ the corrections of a time protocol; `tai` is `realtime` without leap seconds;
※※ the two `_coarse` ones are cheaper and coarser; and `process_cputime` and
※※ `thread_cputime` count the processor time of the process and of the thread.
@[export]
enum Clock : i32:
    realtime = 0 ;
    monotonic = 1 ;
    process_cputime = 2 ;
    thread_cputime = 3 ;
    monotonic_raw = 4 ;
    realtime_coarse = 5 ;
    monotonic_coarse = 6 ;
    boottime = 7 ;
    tai = 11

※ Where the two are in the vDSO, nought for one not there.
type Found = gettime : u64 ; getres : u64

※ Where the vDSO's two functions are, found before the program starts by a
※ walk of a few dozen symbols, once for both.  Its initializer runs only where
※ something kept reads one of the two below, so a program reading no clock
※ neither runs it nor holds it.
constinit let found: Found = look(⎕auxv(33) ?? 0u64)

※ The vDSO's two functions, which take and answer what the system calls do:
※ nought, or the error negated.  Nought where there is none.
constinit let vdso_gettime: @[cdecl, impure] fn(i32, &mut Timespec) → i32 = (
    ⎕at(found.gettime, ⌜@[cdecl, impure] fn(i32, &mut Timespec) → i32⌝))

constinit let vdso_getres: @[cdecl, impure] fn(i32, &mut Timespec) → i32 = (
    ⎕at(found.getres, ⌜@[cdecl, impure] fn(i32, &mut Timespec) → i32⌝))

※ What an image of 64 bits says, read where it lies.  Every address below is
※ worked out wrapping: it is one the kernel mapped, and a check that could
※ stop the program would bring all that stopping takes into every image.
fn u64_at(at: u64) → u64:
    ⎕at(at, ⌜&u64⌝)⌖

fn u32_at(at: u64) → u64:
    ⎕widen(⎕at(at, ⌜&u32⌝)⌖, ⌜u64⌝)

fn u16_at(at: u64) → u64:
    ⎕widen(⎕at(at, ⌜&u16⌝)⌖, ⌜u64⌝)

※ Whether the text of a string table at `at` is `name`, nothing following it.
@[inline(never)]
fn named(at: u64, name: str) → bool:
    let here: mut u64 = at
    foreach byte: u8 = ⎕bytes(name):
        if ⎕at(here, ⌜&u8⌝)⌖ ≠ byte:
            return false
        here ← ⎕wrap(here + 1u64)
    ⎕at(here, ⌜&u8⌝)⌖ = 0u8

※ The two functions in the dynamic symbols of the vDSO at `image`, none where
※ `image` is nought, as it is where the vector names no vDSO.  The kernel
※ names them `__vdso_…` on x86-64 and RISC-V and `__kernel_…` on AArch64, and
※ no image has both, so either name is taken.  How many symbols there are is
※ read from the SysV hash table, which the kernel links every vDSO with
※ (`--hash-style=sysv` on AArch64, `both` on the other two); one without it is
※ taken to have none of the two.
fn look(image: u64) → Found:
    let none: Found = Found(.gettime ← 0u64, .getres ← 0u64)
    if image = 0u64:
        return none
    let phoff: u64 = u64_at(⎕wrap(image + 32u64))
    let phentsize: u64 = u16_at(⎕wrap(image + 54u64))
    let bias: mut u64 = 0u64
    let loaded: mut bool = false
    let dynamic: mut u64 = 0u64
    foreach at: u64 = 0u64…u16_at(⎕wrap(image + 56u64)):
        let header: u64 = ⎕wrap(image + phoff + at × phentsize)
        let kind: u64 = u32_at(header)
        if kind = 1u64 ∧ ¬loaded:
            ※ Where the image is, against where it says it was linked.
            bias ← ⎕wrap(image + u64_at(header + 8u64) - u64_at(header + 16u64))
            loaded ← true
        elif kind = 2u64:
            dynamic ← u64_at(⎕wrap(header + 16u64))
    if ¬loaded ∨ dynamic = 0u64:
        return none
    let strings: mut u64 = 0u64
    let symbols: mut u64 = 0u64
    let hash: mut u64 = 0u64
    let entry: mut u64 = ⎕wrap(dynamic + bias)
    while u64_at(entry) ≠ 0u64:
        let tag: u64 = u64_at(entry)
        let where: u64 = ⎕wrap(u64_at(entry + 8u64) + bias)
        if tag = 5u64:
            strings ← where
        elif tag = 6u64:
            symbols ← where
        elif tag = 4u64:
            hash ← where
        entry ← ⎕wrap(entry + 16u64)
    if strings = 0u64 ∨ symbols = 0u64 ∨ hash = 0u64:
        return none
    let count: u64 = u32_at(⎕wrap(hash + 4u64))
    let found: mut Found = none
    foreach at: u64 = 1u64…count:
        let symbol: u64 = ⎕wrap(symbols + 24u64 × at)
        ※ Defined, and a function.
        if u16_at(⎕wrap(symbol + 6u64)) ≠ 0u64 ∧ (u32_at(⎕wrap(symbol + 4u64)) & 15u64) = 2u64:
            let name: u64 = ⎕wrap(strings + u32_at(symbol))
            let address: u64 = ⎕wrap(u64_at(symbol + 8u64) + bias)
            if named(name, "__vdso_clock_gettime") ∨ named(name, "__kernel_clock_gettime"):
                found.gettime ← address
            elif named(name, "__vdso_clock_getres") ∨ named(name, "__kernel_clock_getres"):
                found.getres ← address
    found

※ The number the kernel gives `clock`, which is how a `Clock` is held.
fn number(clock: Clock) → i32:
    ⎕at(⎕address(&clock), ⌜&i32⌝)⌖

※ The time or the resolution of what a call answered, or its error number.
fn answer(got: i64, into: &Timespec) → Timespec ? i32:
    if got < 0i64:
        return ⊥ ⎕narrow(⎕wrap(0i64 - got), ⌜i32⌝) ?? 22i32
    into⌖

※※ What time `clock` says it is: POSIX's `clock_gettime`.
※※
※※ \param clock which clock to read
※※ \return the time, or the system's error number where the clock cannot be
※※ read -- 22 (`EINVAL`) for one this system does not keep
@[export, impure]
fn clock_gettime(clock: Clock) → Timespec ? i32:
    let into: mut Timespec = Timespec(.tv_sec ← 0i64, .tv_nsec ← 0i64)
    let got: i64 = if ⎕address(vdso_gettime) ≠ 0u64:
        ⎕widen(vdso_gettime(number(clock), &mut into), ⌜i64⌝)
    else:
        ⎕syscall(⎕sc@clock_gettime, number(clock), &mut into)
    answer(got, &into)

※※ How finely `clock` tells the time: POSIX's `clock_getres`.
※※
※※ \param clock which clock to ask
※※ \return the smallest step the clock takes, or the system's error number
@[export, impure]
fn clock_getres(clock: Clock) → Timespec ? i32:
    let into: mut Timespec = Timespec(.tv_sec ← 0i64, .tv_nsec ← 0i64)
    let got: i64 = if ⎕address(vdso_getres) ≠ 0u64:
        ⎕widen(vdso_getres(number(clock), &mut into), ⌜i64⌝)
    else:
        ⎕syscall(⎕sc@clock_getres, number(clock), &mut into)
    answer(got, &into)
